Let $(R, I)$ be a henselian pair of $\mathbb{F}_p$-algebras. Then for each $n$, the map $
u^n(R) 	o 
u^n(R/I)$ is surjective and the map $\widetilde{
u}^{n}(R) 	o \widetilde{
u}^n(R/I)$ is an isomorphism.

We use the commutative diagram \[ \xymatrix{ V_0 \ar[d] \ar[r] & V_1 \ar[d] \ \Omega^n_R \ar[r]^-{1 - C^{-1}} \ar[d] & \Omega^n_R/ d \Omega^{n-1}_R \ar[d] \ \Omega^n_{R/I} \ar[r]^-{1 - C^{-1}} & \Omega^n_{R/I}/ d \Omega^{n-1}_{R/I} }\] where we define $V_0, V_1$ to be the kernels of the surjective vertical maps $\Omega^n_R 	o \Omega^n_{R/I}$ and $\Omega^n_R/ d \Omega^{n-1}_R 	o \Omega^n_{R/I}/ d \Omega^{n-1}_{R/I}$. We will show that the map $V_0 	o V_1$ is surjective. This easily implies the desired conclusions about the maps $
u^n(R) 	o 
u^{n}(R/I)$ and $\widetilde{
u}^n(R) 	o \widetilde{
u}^n(R/I)$ thanks to the snake lemma. Consider a differential form $\omega = a dx_1 dx_2 \dots dx_n \in \Omega^n_R$ such that one of $\left\{a, x_1, \dots, x_night\}$ belongs to $I$ (here $n = 0$ is allowed). The image of such a class in $\Omega^n_R/d \Omega_R^{n-1}$ belongs to $V_1$, and $V_1$ is generated by such classes. For $u \in R$, we have $$(1 - C^{-1}) ( u \omega) = (u - u^p a^{p-1} x_1^{p-1} \dots x_n^{p-1}) \omega.$$ Since $(R, I)$ is a henselian pair and $a^{p-1} x_1^{p-1} \dots x_n^{p-1}\in I$, we can choose $u \in R$ such that $u - u^p a^{p-1} x_1^{p-1} \dots x_n^{p-1} = 1$, using Lemma~ef{solveeq:hens}. The class $u \omega \in \Omega^n_R$ belongs to $V_0$ and has image given by $\omega$; so $\omega$ is in the image of $V_0 	o V_1$, as desired.

We use the commutative diagram \[ \xymatrix{ V_0 \ar[d] \ar[r] & V_1 \ar[d] \ \Omega^n_R \ar[r]^-{1 - C^{-1}} \ar[d] & \Omega^n_R/ d \Omega^{n-1}_R \ar[d] \ \Omega^n_{R/I} \ar[r]^-{1 - C^{-1}} & \Omega^n_{R/I}/ d \Omega^{n-1}_{R/I} }\] where we define $V_0, V_1$ to be the kernels of the surjective vertical maps $\Omega^n_R 	o \Omega^n_{R/I}$ and $\Omega^n_R/ d \Omega^{n-1}_R 	o \Omega^n_{R/I}/ d \Omega^{n-1}_{R/I}$. We will show that the map $V_0 	o V_1$ is surjective. This easily implies the desired conclusions about the maps $
u^n(R) 	o 
u^{n}(R/I)$ and $\widetilde{
u}^n(R) 	o \widetilde{
u}^n(R/I)$ thanks to the snake lemma.

Let $R$ be any local $ b F_p$-algebra, $n\ge-1$, and $r\ge 1$. Then the trace map $\pi_n (K(R)/p^r) 	o \pi_n (\TC(R)/p^r)$ is split injective. More precisely, there is a functor $\widetilde{
u^{n+1}_r}:\operatorname{CAlg}_{\mathbb{F}_p}ightarrow\operatorname{Ab}$ and a natural transformation $\widetilde{
u^{n+1}_r}(-)ightarrow \pi_n(\TC(-)/p^r)$ such that, for $R$ local, the induced map $$\widetilde{
u^{n+1}_r}(R)\oplus \pi_n(K(R)/p^r)\To\pi_n(\TC(R)/p^r)$$ is an isomorphism. This is also compatible with the natural transition maps as $r$ varies. Moreover, $\widetilde{
u^{n+1}_r}$ commutes with filtered colimits; in particular, the direct sum decomposition above holds for arbitrary $R$ after Zariski sheafification.

conclusions:
there is a functor $\widetilde{
u^{n+1}_r}:\operatorname{CAlg}_{\mathbb{F}_p}ightarrow\operatorname{Ab}$ and a natural transformation $\widetilde{
u^{n+1}_r}(-)ightarrow \pi_n(\TC(-)/p^r)$ such that, for $R$ local, the induced map $\widetilde{
u^{n+1}_r}(R)\oplus \pi_n(K(R)/p^r)\To\pi_n(\TC(R)/p^r)$ is an isomorphism.

nodes.statement:
Recall that if $R$ is an $\mathbb{F}_p$-algebra, we can make a functorial surjection to $R$ from a polynomial algebra, namely take the polynomial algebra $\mathbb{F}_p[x_a]_{a\in R}$ on variables indexed by the elements of $R$ with the map $x_a\mapsto a$. Let $R'ightarrow R$ denote the henselization of this surjection at its kernel; thus $R'$ is a functorial ind-smooth $\mathbb{F}_p$-algebra surjecting onto $R$ with henselian kernel. Moreover, $R\mapsto R'$ evidently commutes with filtered colimits. Define for $m \geq 0$ $$\widetilde{
u^m_r}(R) := H^1(\operatorname{Spec}(R')_\sub{'{e}t};\pi_{m}(\mathcal{TC}/p^r)).$$ Since the coefficient presheaf $\pi_{m}(\TC(-)/p^r)$ commutes with filtered colimits by Corollary ef{TCring}, this 'etale cohomology group commutes with filtered colimits in $R'$ by standard cocontinuity arguments. Combining with the previous, we find that $\widetilde{
u^m_r}(-)$ commutes with filtered colimits. Furthermore, since $\operatorname{Spec}(R')$ has '{e}tale $p$-cohomological dimension $\leq 1$, the descent spectral sequence for $\TC(-)/p^r$ gives a natural map $\widetilde{
u^{n+1}_r}(R)ightarrow \pi_n(\TC(R')/p^r)$ (compare with the proofs of Theorem ef{GHGL} and Theorem ef{padicetaleKthy}). Composing with the map on $\TC$ induced by $R'ightarrow R$ defines the desired natural transformation $\widetilde{
u^{n+1}_r}(R)ightarrow \pi_n(\TC(R)/p^r)$. Now assume $R$ is local. Then $R'$ is too, since a henselian ideal is radical, and it is moreover ind-smooth. Thus the argument recalled before the statement of the theorem, based on the results of Geisser--Levine and Geisser--Hesselholt, shows that $$\widetilde{
u^{n+1}_r}(R)\oplus \pi_n(K(R')/p^r)\overset{\sim}{ightarrow}\pi_n(\TC(R')/p^r).$$ On the other hand our main rigidity theorem, Theorem ef{mainthm}, gives a long exact sequence $$\ldotsightarrow\pi_n(K(R')/p^r)ightarrow \pi_n(\TC(R')/p^r)\oplus \pi_n(K(R)/p^r)ightarrow \pi_n(\TC(R)/p^r)ightarrow\ldots.$$ Combining shows both that this long exact sequence breaks up into short exact sequences and that $$\widetilde{
u^{n+1}_r}(R)\oplus\pi_n(K(R)/p^r)\overset{\sim}{ightarrow}\pi_n(\TC(R)/p^r),$$ as claimed.
